          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is the difference between E11 and E11.9, and when is each the right thing to quote?",
          "answer": "**E11 is a category heading, not a code to report on its own.** The coding summary says E11 \"is never used at three characters.\" Its fourth and later characters name the complication, such as kidney involvement, retinal involvement, neuropathy, or circulation problems in the limbs. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*.)\n\n**E11.9 is the fourth-character code for type 2 diabetes documented without complications.** It is the code quoted most often for that situation. (Same source.)\n\nSome points from the same source:\n\n- **Type 2 is the default.** If a record says only \"diabetes mellitus\" with no type, it is classified as type 2.\n- **Insulin does not change the category.** Someone with type 2 diabetes who takes insulin is still coded to E11. Long-term insulin use is recorded as a separate status code.\n\nThe retrieved material does not spell out the full rules for choosing between E11.9 and the complication codes in a particular record. Which code applies depends on what the clinician documented, so a trained medical coder or the treating clinician should make that decision.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
